# Sidebar

The shell's section navigation: one entry per top-level product
area, collapsible to icons.

## When to use

- Once, in the application layout — it is the product's map. Pages
  supply nothing; the layout passes `nav_items`.

## When not to use

- Within-page navigation — `tabs`, accordion or in-page links (see
  the *Navigation* pattern page).
- Deep hierarchies — the sidebar holds one level; if a section
  needs sub-navigation, that belongs inside the section's pages.

## Usage

`render_sidebar` (`Gesso::Components::SidebarHelper`) derives the active section
from the request path and renders the navigation. Call it once, in the
application layout.

```erb
<%%= render_sidebar(
  app_name: "NHS App",
  nav_items: [
    { label: "Dashboard", path: "/",         icon: "layout-dashboard" },
    { label: "Patients",  path: "/patients", icon: "users" }
  ]
) %>
```

<%= embed :sidebar, :default %>

| Param | Notes |
| ----- | ----- |
| `app_name` | product name beside the logo mark |
| `nav_items` | `{ label:, path:, icon: }` hashes, one per section |

The helper derives the active item from `request.path` (prefix match;
exact for `/`) and marks it with `aria-current="page"`, which styles it
with the `--sidebar-primary` blue-violet accent in both themes (see
*Colour* under Foundations). Collapse behaviour comes from its Stimulus
`sidebar` controller.

## Do and don't

- Do keep it to the sections a user thinks of as *places* — around
  seven entries; past that, the product wants regrouping, not a
  longer list.
- Do give each section its established lucide icon — icons are what
  remains when collapsed.
- Don't put actions in the sidebar; it navigates, it never mutates.
- Don't reorder sections per page or per role without strong need —
  spatial memory is the sidebar's value.

## Accessibility and content

- Real `<nav>` with a list of links; the current section carries
  `aria-current="page"`. When collapsed the visible label is hidden but
  each link keeps its accessible name via an `aria-label`, so screen
  readers are unaffected by collapse.
- Labels are one or two words, sentence case, matching the page
  titles they lead to.
